<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">skills</ansi>

skills lists your trained skill ranks. train [skill] raises one rank;
these ranks are the inputs to your abilities, not separate unlocks for
every capability they support.

In the web client, Character > Skills gives each trained skill a card
with its rank out of its real maximum (the pips and "2/4") and a line on
what it does; click one for its help page. Below them come automatic
combat abilities, field capabilities and camp capabilities, each with
what it does and whether it is ready or why not. Retired skills are no
longer listed. The web client does not show the jobs the jobs command
lists: your class is what names you here (help classes).

Automatic combat abilities act when their conditions hold, rather than
waiting for a manual command. The panel shows their mechanical effect,
conditions and cooldown in combat rounds. strategy [member] abilities
on|off controls class abilities. Automatic spells instead depend on the
strategy role, learned spell, cast rank, mana and attack mana reserve.
See <ansi fg="command">help abilities</ansi> and <ansi fg="command">help strategy</ansi>.

Field and camp capabilities use your current archetype and its trained
skill rank. One rank can support several capabilities; do not train each
capability separately. autoskill [utility] on|off controls these jobs.
Your company's best eligible member here does the work when the job's
conditions hold. Eligible does not mean a job has already happened:
light needs darkness, camp rewards need a completed rest, and forage
needs a configured local food table and cargo space.
See <ansi fg="command">help specialists</ansi> and <ansi fg="command">help autoskill</ansi>.
Camp Cooking is manual and class-independent: camp cook uses configured
recipe ranks and ingredients at your lit campfire outside battle.
It is separate from autoskills (<ansi fg="command">help cooking</ansi>).
Scribe is a caster skill that reads the hidden affixes of rare gear: wizards
and clerics train it, and it reads for the whole company at camp
(<ansi fg="command">help scribe</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Companions' skills</ansi>

A companion's skills come in two kinds. Class skills are automatic: its
archetype and level give it its specialist skills
(<ansi fg="command">help specialists</ansi>).
Optional skills, Cooking and Scribe as the world ships, are trained: a companion
earns training points by level, and you spend them with
<ansi fg="command">company train</ansi>. Character > Skills lists each companion's
optional skills and points, read only. See <ansi fg="command">help company train</ansi>.

Usage: <ansi fg="command">skills</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">train [skill]</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">company train</ansi>
See also: <ansi fg="command">help archetype</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help webclient</ansi>.
